\newcommand{\sq}{\sigma}
\title{Constant square counts on sixteen vertices}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
For an edge $xy$ of a simple triangle-free graph, let $\sq(xy)$ be the
number of cycles of length four containing that edge. Equivalently,
$\sq(xy)$ is the number of edges between $N(x)\setminus\{y\}$ and
$N(y)\setminus\{x\}$. These neighbourhoods are disjoint. We prove a
classification at one order and deduce three lower bounds recorded in
Table~4 of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
Let $G$ be a simple triangle-free graph with sixteen vertices, all of
degree six. If $\sq$ is constant on its edges, then this constant is
seventeen and $G$ is obtained from $K_{8,8}$ by deleting four disjoint
copies of $K_{2,2}$.
\end{theorem}

We use two elementary facts from the preceding argument for the case
$\sq=17$~\cite{dense}. First, if a triangle-free $k$-regular graph with
$n$ vertices has an odd cycle, its shortest odd cycle has length
$\ell$ satisfying $k\ell\le2n$. Indeed, every vertex has at most two
neighbours on that cycle. A third neighbour would give cyclic gaps
of length at least two, one of them odd; that gap and the outside
vertex would form a shorter odd cycle. Counting incidences with the
cycle proves the inequality.

Second, in a bipartite graph of degree six with parts of size eight,
write $H$ for its complement in $K_{8,8}$. Then $H$ has degree two and,
for every edge $xy$ of $G$,
\begin{equation}\label{complement}
 \sq(xy)=17-p_H(x,y),
\end{equation}
where $p_H(x,y)$ counts walks of length three in $H$ from $x$ to $y$.
Since $xy$ is not an edge of $H$, such walks have distinct vertices.
For reference, if $x,t$ belong to the same part and
$\mu(x,t)=|N_H(x)\cap N_H(t)|$, then
$\sum_{t\ne x}\mu(x,t)=2$ and
\[
 \sq(xy)=\sum_{t\in N_G(y)\setminus\{x\}}(3+\mu(x,t))
 =17-\sum_{t\in N_H(y)}\mu(x,t).
\]
This proves~\eqref{complement}. We recall these facts to specify precisely
the common material used below.

\begin{lemma}\label{nonbip}
A triangle-free graph of degree six on sixteen vertices which contains
an odd cycle cannot have a constant square count on its edges.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The preceding incidence bound forces a shortest odd cycle of length
five. Write it as $C=(v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_4)$, with indices modulo five,
and partition the whole vertex set by its neighbours on $C$:
\[
 A_i=\{x:N(x)\cap V(C)=\{v_{i-1},v_{i+1}\}\},\quad
 B_i=\{x:N(x)\cap V(C)=\{v_i\}\},
\]
with $Z$ containing the vertices with no neighbour on $C$. Thus
$v_i\in A_i$. Put $a_i=|A_i|$, $b_i=|B_i|$ and $z=|Z|$.
The cycle degrees and the total number of vertices give
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=6,\qquad
 \sum_i(a_i+b_i)+z=16,\qquad \sum_i b_i+2z=2.
\]
The five equations determining $a$ have a unique solution for each
$b$. Up to rotation and reversal the possible sizes are
\begin{equation}\label{patterns}
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
z&b&a\\\hline
1&(0,0,0,0,0)&(3,3,3,3,3)\\
0&(2,0,0,0,0)&(2,2,4,4,2)\\
0&(1,1,0,0,0)&(2,2,3,4,3)\\
0&(1,0,1,0,0)&(3,2,3,3,3).
\end{array}
\end{equation}
To see completeness, either $z=1$ and $b=0$, or $z=0$ and the two
units of $b$ lie at one position, at consecutive positions, or at
nonconsecutive positions. Substitution gives the displayed rows.

Each $A_i$ is independent, and edges between $A$ classes can occur
only between consecutive classes: any other pair shares a cycle
neighbour. A vertex in $B_i$ can meet only $A_i,A_{i-2},A_{i+2}$
among the $A$ classes. Its only neighbour on the actual cycle is
$v_i$. We use these restrictions throughout. Write $\lambda$ for
the putative constant value of $\sq$.

\paragraph{One vertex outside all cycle neighbourhoods.}
In the first row of~\eqref{patterns}, the graph on the $A$ classes
is obtained from the complete balanced blow-up of $C_5$ by deleting
some edges. Every vertex has six possible neighbours in the two
adjacent classes. It therefore loses exactly one such edge if it is
adjacent to the unique vertex in $Z$, and none otherwise. Since that
vertex has degree six, exactly three edges are deleted. No deleted
edge touches a cycle vertex.

In the complete blow-up, each original cycle edge lies on sixteen
squares. A deleted edge between the noncycle parts of $A_i$ and
$A_{i+1}$ contributes to the opposite-neighbourhood count for exactly
three cycle edges, those indexed $i-1,i,i+1$. The original cycle
neighbourhoods do not change and do not contain the vertex in $Z$.
It follows that
\[
                 5\lambda=5\cdot16-3\cdot3=71,
\]
which is impossible.

\paragraph{Two vertices with the same cycle neighbour.}
In the second row let $B_0=\{u,w\}$. Each vertex of $A_1$ is adjacent
to all of $A_0\cup A_2$, since these are its only six possible
neighbours. Likewise $A_4$ is complete to $A_0\cup A_3$. The vertices
of $A_0$ must then be adjacent to both $u$ and $w$. Each of $u,w$
has these two neighbours in $A_0$ and four further neighbours among
the six noncycle vertices of $A_2\cup A_3$.

Put $P=e(B_0,A_2)$ and $Q=e(B_0,A_3)$. The missing edges between
$A_2$ and $A_3$ number $P$ when counted from $A_2$ and $Q$ when
counted from $A_3$, so $P=Q$. Since $P+Q=8$, both equal four.
For the edge $v_0v_1$, the opposite neighbourhoods are
$A_4\cup(A_1\setminus\{v_1\})\cup B_0$ and
$(A_0\setminus\{v_0\})\cup A_2$. The three sets in the first
neighbourhood contribute respectively $2$, $1+4$, and $2+P$ edges
to the second. Hence $\lambda=9+P=13$.

On the other hand, let $c$ count the extra neighbours shared by $u$
and $w$ in $A_2\cup A_3$. Their two sets of four vertices in a set
of six have $c\ge2$. For $v_0u$, the vertices of $A_1\cup A_4$
contribute twelve opposite-neighbourhood edges: four to the other
vertex of $A_0$, and eight to the four extra neighbours of $u$.
The vertex $w$ contributes $1+c$. Thus $\sq(v_0u)=13+c\ge15$,
a contradiction.

\paragraph{Two consecutive cycle neighbours.}
In the third row write $B_0=\{u\}$, $B_1=\{w\}$, and let
$e\in\{0,1\}$ indicate whether $uw$ is an edge. The six permitted
neighbours force $A_0$ to be complete to $A_4,A_1,\{u\}$, and
$A_1$ to be complete to $A_0,A_2,\{w\}$. Put
\[
 p=|N(u)\cap A_2|,\ q=|N(u)\cap A_3|,\quad
 r=|N(w)\cap A_4|,\ s=|N(w)\cap A_3|.
\]
The cycle vertices are forbidden here, so $p,r\le2$. Degrees give
$p+q+e=r+s+e=4$. The missing edges between $A_2,A_3$ number $p$;
those between $A_4,A_3$ number $r$. Counting the same deficits from
$A_3$ gives $p+r=q+s$, whence $p+r=4-e\ge3$. Both $p,r$ are
therefore positive.

Every vertex of $A_2$ adjacent to $u$ has exactly one missing
neighbour in $A_3$. All $q$ neighbours of $u$ in $A_3$ must be
missing there, by triangle-freeness. Thus $q\le1$, and similarly
$s\le1$. The degree equations now force
$p=r=2$, $q=s=e=1$, contrary to $p+r=4-e=3$.

\paragraph{Two nonconsecutive cycle neighbours.}
In the last row write $B_0=\{u\}$, $B_2=\{w\}$. The class $A_1$
is complete to $A_0\cup A_2$. Set
\[
 q=|N(u)\cap A_2|,\quad s=|N(w)\cap A_0|,\quad
 p=|N(u)\cap(A_0\setminus\{v_0\})|.
\]
All three numbers are at most two. Summing degrees in $A_0$ gives
$e(A_0,A_4)=11-p-s$. In the opposite-neighbourhood count for $v_0v_1$,
the other vertex of $A_1$ contributes five edges, $A_4$ contributes
$8-p-s$, and $u$ contributes $p+q$. Consequently
\[
 \sq(v_0v_1)=13+q-s,\qquad \sq(v_1v_2)=13+s-q.
\]
The second equality is the first with the roles reversed. Constancy
forces $q=s$ and $\lambda=13$.

Define also
\[
 p'=|N(w)\cap(A_2\setminus\{v_2\})|,\quad
 r=|N(u)\cap A_3|,\quad r'=|N(w)\cap A_4|,
\]
and again let $e$ indicate adjacency of $u,w$. Each of
$p,p',q,r,r'$ belongs to $\{0,1,2\}$, and the two degrees give
\begin{equation}\label{degrees}
 p+q+r+e=p'+q+r'+e=5.
\end{equation}
Let $D_{ij}$ count missing edges between $A_i$ and $A_j$. Degree
sums in $A_0,A_2,A_3,A_4$ yield
\begin{equation}\label{deficits}
 D_{04}=p+q-2,\quad D_{23}=p'+q-2,\quad
 D_{34}=r-D_{23}=r'-D_{04}=7-e-p-p'-2q.
\end{equation}
Triangle-freeness at $u$ and $w$ requires
\begin{equation}\label{triangles}
                 qr\le D_{23},\qquad qr'\le D_{04}.
\end{equation}
We finish by considering the three possible values of $q$.

If $q=0$, equations~\eqref{degrees} force
$e=1$ and $p=p'=r=r'=2$. In~\eqref{deficits}, $D_{04}=D_{23}=0$
and $D_{34}=2$. The neighbours of $u$ other than $w$ are all of
$A_0$ and the two noncycle vertices of $A_3$; those of $w$ other
than $u$ are all of $A_2$ and the two noncycle vertices of $A_4$.
Their cross edges number
\[
                   6+6+(4-2)=14.
\]
The two missing $A_3$--$A_4$ edges are both between noncycle vertices,
since the cycle neighbourhoods are fixed. Thus $\sq(uw)=14\ne13$.

If $q=1$, inequalities~\eqref{triangles} give
$r\le p'-1\le1$ and $r'\le p-1\le1$. Equations~\eqref{degrees}
force $e=1$, $p=p'=2$ and $r=r'=1$. Then $u,w$ share a noncycle
neighbour in $A_0$, contradicting the absence of a triangle on $uw$.

If $q=2$, inequalities~\eqref{triangles} give
$2r\le p'\le2$ and $2r'\le p\le2$, so $r,r'\le1$.
If $e=1$, the two neighbours of $w$ in $A_0\setminus\{v_0\}$
force $p=0$ to avoid a triangle on $uw$. The first degree equation
then gives $r=2$, a contradiction. Hence $e=0$ and the degree
equations force $p=p'=2$, $r=r'=1$. But then $D_{34}=-1$.
All four size patterns have now been excluded.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
By Lemma~\ref{nonbip}, the graph is bipartite. Regularity makes the
parts equal, so both have size eight. Its bipartite complement $H$
is a union of even cycles of length at least four. If $H$ has two
or more components, choose $x,y$ in opposite parts and different
components. They are adjacent in $G$, but $p_H(x,y)=0$, so
\eqref{complement} forces $\lambda=17$. A component of $H$ of
length at least six would contain a three-edge path whose endpoints
are not adjacent in $H$, giving a positive value of $p_H$ and a
contradiction. Thus all components are four-cycles.

If $H$ has only one component, it is $C_{16}$. Two vertices at cyclic
distance three have a positive value of $p_H$, whereas two vertices
at cyclic distance five have value zero. Both pairs are edges of
$G$, again contradicting constancy. Only four disjoint four-cycles
remain. Conversely, deleting these from $K_{8,8}$ gives degree six,
and every remaining edge joins distinct components of $H$.
Formula~\eqref{complement} gives exactly seventeen squares on each
edge. This proves existence and uniqueness.
\end{proof}

Let $n(k,4,\lambda)$ denote the least order of a graph of degree $k$,
girth four and constant square count $\lambda$, when such graphs exist;
the following inequalities also hold if that least order is infinite.
\begin{corollary}\label{bounds}
The following three bounds hold:
\[
 n(6,4,13)\ge20,\qquad n(6,4,14)\ge18,\qquad n(6,4,15)\ge20.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
In a triangle-free $k$-regular graph the two open neighbourhoods of
an edge are disjoint, so $n\ge2k$. Counting all incidences between
edges and squares gives $8\mid nk\lambda$. For degree six, the
possible orders below the displayed bounds are therefore
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda&\text{orders requiring exclusion}\\\hline
13&12,16\\
14&12,14,16\\
15&12,16.
\end{array}
\]
At orders twelve and fourteen the shortest-odd-cycle inequality
excludes every odd cycle, since $6\cdot5>2n$. The bipartite graphs
are respectively $K_{6,6}$ and $K_{7,7}$ with a perfect matching
deleted. Their constant square counts are $25$ and $20$.
At order sixteen, Theorem~\ref{main} allows only the value $17$.
This excludes every order in the table.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and verification.}
The three numerical bounds in Corollary~\ref{bounds} are the lower
bounds of Table~4 on physical page~20 of~\cite{GJ}; they are not
claimed as new open-problem resolutions. The cycle partition and
complement identity were used in the preceding proof~\cite{dense}.
The present argument removes the assumption $\lambda=17$ by treating
all four cycle partitions exactly. This describes the contribution
of this component, without a claim of historical priority.

The accompanying finite checks verify the partition equations,
local square-count identities, the parameter restrictions in the
last two cases, and all possible cycle decompositions of $H$.
They check the explicit positive graph and perturbed negative examples.
The classification follows from the complete written proof, rather
than from a graph census. No additional Lean formalization is claimed.

